#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106771} :activity/id #uuid "0a79b6d7-a30d-5f46-addd-9ad83b0960c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105659 :question/id #uuid "9bdb609d-4000-5ee3-9267-71ed6a8dd140" :question/math-academy-id "e-7392"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357394}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"} :step/id #uuid "4e3d714f-5ee5-588e-a541-2b56e8ec5909" :step/math-academy-id 27400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105660 :question/id #uuid "cbc5bcb5-2c64-5895-ba1c-cc6d86207667" :question/math-academy-id "e-7393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354340}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "2e6311d2-17da-5bd4-a8bb-025d853635b4" :step/math-academy-id 27401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107105 :tutorial/content "A **binomial distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (or trials) where each outcome is either success or failure. In a binomial distribution, the probability of success in each experiment is the same.\n\nIf there are $n$ experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of successes has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2, \\ldots, n\n$$\n\nFor example, if we flip a coin $5$ times, then the number of heads follows a binomial distribution.\n\n- Each toss is an experiment that either results in success (i.e., heads) or failure (i.e., tails).\n\n- There are $n=5$ experiments.\n\n- Each experiment has a probability $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of success.\n\nSo, the number of heads has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(\\frac{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution consisting of $n$ experiments (or trials) and the probability of success on each trial is $p,$ we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim B(n,p).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn words, we say, \"the random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution with $n$ trials and probability $p$ of success on each trial.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "7e7bad04-ccb9-574b-9792-eb4b8a51b62b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4451 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf257099-cebd-5c9d-9671-6b14b1f63d0d" :step/math-academy-id 27402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105661 :question/id #uuid "1fab59f6-f640-5533-9bff-4946dfd71f77" :question/math-academy-id "e-7394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites